Here's a complete example of a C# MCP server wrapping a REST API with stdio transport.

---

### **Program.cs** — stdio transport with DI

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

var builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);

// CRITICAL: Log to stderr only — stdout is the JSON-RPC channel
builder.Logging.AddConsole(options =>
    options.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace);

builder.Services.AddHttpClient("api", client =>
{
    client.BaseAddress = new Uri("https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com");
});

builder.Services.AddMcpServer()
    .WithStdioServerTransport()
    .WithToolsFromAssembly();

await builder.Build().RunAsync();
```

---

### **ApiTools.cs** — tool class wrapping a REST API

```csharp
using System.ComponentModel;
using System.Text.Json;
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;

[McpServerToolType]
public class ApiTools(IHttpClientFactory httpFactory, ILogger<ApiTools> logger)
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("Get a post by its ID from the API.")]
    public async Task<string> GetPost(
        [Description("The numeric post ID (1-100)")] int postId,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Fetching post {PostId}", postId);
        var client = httpFactory.CreateClient("api");
        return await client.GetStringAsync($"/posts/{postId}", cancellationToken);
    }

    [McpServerTool, Description("List all posts by a specific user.")]
    public async Task<string> GetPostsByUser(
        [Description("The user ID to filter by")] int userId,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        var client = httpFactory.CreateClient("api");
        return await client.GetStringAsync($"/posts?userId={userId}", cancellationToken);
    }

    [McpServerTool(Destructive = false, ReadOnly = true),
     Description("Search posts by title keyword.")]
    public async Task<string> SearchPosts(
        [Description("Keyword to search in post titles")] string keyword,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        var client = httpFactory.CreateClient("api");
        var json = await client.GetStringAsync("/posts", cancellationToken);
        var posts = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<JsonElement>(json);

        var matches = posts.EnumerateArray()
            .Where(p => p.GetProperty("title").GetString()!
                .Contains(keyword, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase))
            .Select(p => p.ToString());

        return $"[{string.Join(",", matches)}]";
    }
}
```

---

### How DI works for MCP tools

The SDK supports **two injection styles**:

| Style | Class type | How services arrive |
|-------|-----------|---------------------|
| **Constructor injection** | Non-static class | Services in the constructor (like `HttpClient`, `ILogger<T>`) |
| **Method parameter injection** | Static class | Services as method params alongside tool params |

**Key rule:** The SDK distinguishes tool parameters from injected services automatically. Types registered in DI (e.g., `IHttpClientFactory`, `ILogger<T>`, `CancellationToken`, `IMcpServer`) are **resolved by the framework** and never appear in the tool's JSON schema exposed to the LLM. Only parameters with `[Description]` are visible to the LLM.

In the example above, `IHttpClientFactory` and `ILogger<ApiTools>` are constructor-injected because `ApiTools` is a **non-static class**. The SDK creates an instance per invocation, resolving dependencies from the DI container. `CancellationToken` is also auto-injected.

To scaffold this project:

```bash
dotnet new mcpserver -n MyApiServer
# then replace the generated tool class and Program.cs
dotnet build && dotnet run
```
